==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=Clinker brick}}
[[File:Hamburg.Reemtsma.Details.wmt.jpg|thumb|'''clinker''' brick]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{sense|brick, vowel}} {{l|en|klinker}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/klɪŋkə/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪŋkə(r)|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|nl|en}} {{m|nl|klinkaerd}}, later {{m|nl|klinker}}, from {{m|nl|klinken||to ring, resound}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A very hard [[brick]] used for [[paving]] customarily made in the [[Netherlands]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* '''1938''', [[w:Xavier Herbert|Xavier Herbert]], ''[[w:Capricornia (novel)|Capricornia]]'', New York: D. Appleton-Century, 1943, Chapter XXXII, p. 581, [https://archive.org/details/capricornianovel00herb]
#*: She left the road at the little shed where he whom she still regarded as her father used to keep his tricycle, and walked up the '''clinker''' path towards the house.
# A mass of bricks fused together by intense heat. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# [[slag|Slag]] or [[ash]] produced by intense heat in a [[furnace]], [[kiln]] or [[boiler]] that forms a hard residue upon cooling. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* '''1944''', {{w|Emily Carr}}, ''{{w|The House of All Sorts}}'', "Dew and Alarm Clocks," [http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks01/0100121h.html]
#*: Cold and grim sat that malevolent brute the furnace, greedy, bottomless—its grate bars clenched over '''clinkers''' which no shaker could dislodge.
# An [[intermediate]] [[product]] in the manufacture of [[Portland cement]], obtained by [[sinter|sintering]] limestone and alumino-silicate materials such as clay into [[nodule|nodules]] in a cement kiln.
# Hardened [[volcanic]] [[lava]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* '''2004''', {{w|Richard Fortey}}, ''The Earth'', Folio Society 2011, p. 10:
#*: Nobody could pretend that a huge slope of '''clinker''' is aesthetically pleasing.
# A [[scum]] of [[oxide]] of [[iron]] formed in [[forging]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}

